Precondition 1 (process/concurrency shape): confirmed the concurrent
Swarm.main() pattern found in the vendored ARC-AGI-3-Agents copy also
governs the REAL scored submission, not just the offline reference --
read scripts/kaggle/submission_kernel/main.py end to end: on a
competition rerun it copies the competition-provided framework, registers
CarnotAgent alongside the stock Swarm import, and runs that framework's
own main.py against the gateway with one scorecard covering the whole
game roster (12h timeout, one process).

Precondition 2 (competition rules on cross-game state): searched the
public ARC Prize / Kaggle documentation directly (docs.arcprize.org
index/full-play-test/methodology/agents-quickstart/scorecards + the main
competition page; the Kaggle rules tab itself is login-gated and
unreachable). Found no explicit rule permitting or forbidding cross-game
memory. This silence is the finding -- not resolved either way, and the
scope now states explicitly that implementation requires operator
sign-off, offering three legitimate paths (approve on the harness-
architecture evidence, ask ARC Prize/Kaggle support directly, or decline
and stay per-game-only) rather than picking one unilaterally.

Still scope only -- nothing implemented.

